# ---------------- 3.2
S32 = """### 3.2 One batch = {body} operations for 256 trails
Representation: a 32-bit word is 32 planes; plane j is a 256-bit integer whose bit l is bit j of the word of lane l. A rotation is a renaming of planes (no operation). XOR, AND, OR of words are 32 operations each. The sum of two words is a ripple of full adders over the planes; the lowest bit has 2 gates (no carry in) and the highest 2 (no carry out), and constants (the IV words, the zero words m0..m7, the length and the flag word) are folded at build time wherever the operand is a constant.
The full adder is the pivot adder of 6ae36351 (our re-implementation, `Gen.fa`). Each signal is a pair (node, polarity): the register holds the node's value and the true plane is that value XOR polarity, so complements are free inside a segment. Of three variable inputs a, b, c, two have the same polarity (pigeonhole); take the third as the pivot p and the two equal ones as x and y. With u = x XOR p and v = y XOR p: sum = u XOR y, carry = (u AND v) XOR p, because maj(x, y, p) = p XOR ((x XOR p) AND (y XOR p)). u and v have equal polarity, so the AND is always one gate (an AND of two upright planes, or an OR of two inverted planes, with the result's polarity set); every full adder with three variable inputs is 5 gates (XOR for u, XOR for the sum, XOR for v, the AND, XOR for the carry). With one constant input, sum = x XOR y XOR k and carry = x AND y or x OR y (2 or 3 gates); with two the plane passes through.
**The rolled program** (`listing` in the program; registers: x and output planes 0..255, state words 256..767, message slots 768..1279, temporaries of the loop body from 1312). One step executes, in this order:
(A) Rounds 1 and 2 as straight-line code with the constants folded: {r1} + {r2} = {Agates} gates. Round 1 is cheap because the state is constant and m0..m7 = 0; from round 2 on every plane depends on x (a straight-line round costs {r2} gates). The 512 state planes are written into the registers 256..767.
(B) A plane that the generator stores inverted (polarity 1) gets one complement, one operation each: {nots} operations. This puts all 512 state planes upright, which the loop body needs: its input and output polarity must be equal in every iteration, and for upright inputs the pivot rule produces only upright outputs (the generator checks it: no output of the body has polarity 1).
(C) The message slots of round 3: the 16 slots of 32 planes are set up in registers 768..1279 as the message words of the third round (slot s holds word P^2[s] of 0^32 || x): 256 planes set to zero and 256 planes copied from the x planes: {winit} operations.
(D) The round loop, 5 iterations (rounds 3 to 7). The body is ONE generic round of {Bn} gates with no constant operand: all 16 message slots are real operands, so the 8 zero words are not folded (a folded round has {r2} gates, the generic one {Bn}: 1,232 more per round, charged in each of the 5 iterations). The state words are read from and written to the registers 256..767 in place (the 512 output planes of the body are pinned to the registers of the 512 input planes; the generator checks that no input plane is still needed when its register is written), the message operands are read from the slot registers 768..1279 and are not changed by the gates.
(E) The message permutation between iterations. The BLAKE3 permutation of the message words is PERM = (2, 6, 3, 10, 7, 0, 4, 13, 1, 11, 12, 5, 9, 14, 15, 8): slot s takes the content of slot PERM[s]. It consists of two cycles of 8 slots. It is carried out by plane copies (register moves, one operation each): per cycle one word (32 planes) is saved in a temporary word, 7 words are moved and the saved word is moved into its place: 9 word copies = 288 moves per cycle, {Mn} per permutation. The permutation is executed after iterations 1 to 4 and not after the 5th (the loop leaves before it): 4 x {Mn} = {Mtot} operations. Re-indexing instead of copying is not used: an instruction names its registers, and the one copy of the body, executed 5 times, reads the same slot registers each time; so the moves are real operations and are counted as such.
(F) Loop control, charged: the counter is set (1 operation); at the end of each iteration it is decremented, compared with zero and a conditional branch leaves the loop when it is zero (3 operations, 5 times); an unconditional jump to the start of the body (1 operation, 4 times): {loopc} operations. The five loop-control records are instructions of 36 bytes like the others.
(G) The digest: for each of the 256 digest planes j, state plane j XOR state plane j + 256 (word i XOR word i + 8, plane by plane), written into register j: {Fn} operations (these are the output planes of the step and the input planes of the next batch).
Executed per step: {Agates} + {nots} + {winit} + 1 + 5 x ({Bn} + 3) + 4 x ({Mn} + 1) + {Fn} = **{PH} operations** ({xorn} XOR, {andn} AND, {orn} OR gates and {movn} complements, copies and zero settings, {ctln} loop-control operations), no other operation. Per trail {perlane:.3f} operations = {unit:.4f} unit, against 1 unit for a compression. The static code of the step has {stepinstr} instructions: A {Alen}, loop control 5, body {Bn}, message permutation {Mn}, digest {Fn}. For comparison the straight-line step of 1cd0dd3b has 45,974 gates and {PH1} instructions: the rolled program saves {savedinstr} instructions of code and costs {PHdiff} more operations per step ({PHpct:.1f} percent).
The mask walk: the output planes are stored in registers 0..255 exactly as the circuit produces them and they are the 256 input planes of the next batch (a chain with no move), and they are upright: q = 0, g = f; the notation of the mask walk (g = f XOR q, equality under g equivalent to equality under f, Section 1) is kept because the recovery circuit and the experiments use it with q = 0.
Validation: the flat listing of the program (all records including the loop control) was executed by an interpreter and by the compiled step against the scalar digest in the selftest; the compiled rolled step against the straight-line step of 1cd0dd3b on {nrolled} lanes (0 differences) and against the organizer reference and the `blake3` library (Section 1).
The generator itself runs once at the start; its work is charged in the setup row (Section 3.3), its code and peak storage are accounted in Section 3.6 and Section 7."""
